# API design rules

## Compatibility is a correctness property
- Find out what is public: installed headers, exported symbols, Python package `__all__`, documented CLI flags and file formats. Everything else is internal.
- Do not change a public signature, default argument, semantics, units, ownership, thread-safety guarantee or error behaviour unless the finding explicitly says it is a breaking change and proposes deprecation first (`[[deprecated("use X")]]`, one release cycle).
- ABI (shared libraries): adding virtual functions, changing class layout or changing inline functions breaks ABI. Note it in the proposal.

## What good looks like
- Sizes and indices as `std::size_t`/`std::int64_t` (or a project index typedef), consistently.
- Non-owning views for array arguments (`std::span<const T>`, pointer + size), with ownership documented. No raw `new`/`delete` across the API boundary.
- Errors: one consistent mechanism per layer (exceptions, `std::expected`-style, or error codes). Never silent failure, never `exit()` in library code.
- Hot-path APIs must not force allocations or virtual dispatch per element. Batch interfaces instead.
- Const-correctness; `noexcept` only where it is guaranteed; `[[nodiscard]]` on returned error states.
- Thread safety documented per function or class.
- Units and conventions (row/column-major, 0/1-based, in/out arguments) documented at the declaration.

## Findings in this category
Each API finding names the concrete misuse it enables or the cost it imposes (e.g. "forces a copy of the 8 GB matrix at solver.hpp:120"). Taste alone isn't a finding.
